# LeakSanitizer suppressions, used by the debug, app and fuzz presets through LSAN_OPTIONS.
#
# Only leaks in code we do not own belong here (a driver, Qt, a system library), one line each
# with the reason above it, in the form `leak:<function, library or file name>`. A leak in our
# own code is fixed, never suppressed.

# Mesa's Vulkan drivers allocate process-wide state once, in pthread_once initialisers, and keep
# it in the driver's static data. When the Vulkan loader unloads the driver at vkDestroyInstance,
# that memory is no longer reachable, and the driver's frames have lost their names. The gpu
# tests' main sets VK_LOADER_DISABLE_DYNAMIC_LIBRARY_UNLOADING=1, which keeps drivers loaded:
# with it, lavapipe and RADV (Mesa 26.2) report nothing. These entries are for a loader that
# ignores the variable. They match the first named frames, the loader entry points that first
# call into lavapipe. The only code of ours below those calls is the debug callback in
# libs/gpu/src/device.cpp, and it keeps nothing.
#
# lavapipe's once-only initialisation, reached when the loader creates the driver's instance.
leak:vkCreateInstance
# The same initialisation, reached when the loader first lists lavapipe's physical devices.
leak:vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices
